    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 220551\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1051 PM MST Mon Sep 21 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 06Z Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry and tranquil conditions, along with near normal \n  temperatures will continue through at least the middle of the \n  week.\n\n- Rain chances will return to portions of Arizona over the next \n  few days, although focused primarily over foothill and high \n  terrain areas.\n\n- Lower desert rainfall chances will be heightened somewhat \n  during the latter half of the week. \n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Wednesday/...\nA seasonally strong trough was moving onshore in southern \nCalifornia early this afternoon with a jet streak translating \ndownstream of the cold core forcing deamplification and lifting of\nthe cyclonic circulation towards the Great Basin. However, jet \nforced ascent has combined with a narrow band of midlevel moisture\nto produce a small area of elevated convection along the lower \nColorado River valley earlier today. This feature will continue to\nexit the forecast area by this evening although residual, \nelongated troughing will linger across southern California/Nevada.\nThis will create a deeply meridional flow pattern over the next \n72 hours capable of importing sheared vorticity centers from the \nsouth, as well as periodically better low level moisture profiles.\nH5 heights will remain nearly steady state in a 580-584dm range \nensuring a continuation of near normal temperatures the next \nseveral days. \n\nBoth global and HREF modeling suggests a low amplitude vorticity \ncenter (likely convectively enhanced over northern Mexico) \nstreaming into eastern Arizona Tuesday afternoon. Along with a \nsmall jet segment feeding into the right rear quadrant of the \nnorthern stream, ascent structure tapping marginally better \nmoisture in SE Arizona will become very favorable for deep \nconvective development over higher terrain locations. While lower \nelevations in the CWA will not initially have sufficient moisture \nfor thunderstorm sustenance, gusty southeast outflow winds seem \nmore likely, fostered by DCape values near 1500 J/kg allowing \nbetter boundary layer moisture to surge northwest through the \nforecast area. As a result, instability parameters will reach much\nhigher levels Wednesday (i.e. MLCape 1000- 1500 J/kg), though \nstronger CinH and lack of a triggering mechanism may preclude more\nthan isolated mountain storms as the region may fall under \nshortwave ridging and subsidence behind the Tuesday vorticity \ncenter exiting the region. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Thursday through Sunday/... \nBy the latter half of the week, the influence of the weakening \nsouthwestern low will all but disappear. The focus will shift \ntoward the interplay between large scale troughing over the \neastern Pacific/West Coast and the subtropical high over the \nSouthern Plains and Northern Mexico. Global ensembles favor \nretrogression toward the Desert Southwest approaching the weekend.\nThis evolution would not completely scour residual moisture \nimmediately, so rain chances will likely linger into Thursday. \nCurrent PoPs show 30-50% chances for foothill and mountain areas \nand 10-30% probabilities for the South- Central Arizona deserts. \nBeyond that, persistent southwesterly flow would eventually dry us\nout enough to limit rain chances heading into the weekend. \nAnother influx of moisture by the end of the seven day period may \nbring rain chances back once again, but max chances at this time \nonly range between 20-30%. \n  \nEven with the competing airmasses, regional temperatures will \nonly fluctuate within a few degrees through the weekend. While the\nridge keeps another shortwave from progressing toward the region,\nas mentioned previously, in return, the large scale troughing to \nour west will keep the high from becoming too amplified, \npreventing any further discussion of abnormal heat, at least \nduring this forecast period. Temperatures for the lower deserts \nThursday through Sunday will range generally between 94-106 \ndegrees, while overnight lows hover near 70-82 degrees. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0551Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \n\nPrimary aviation impact will be the potential for VCSH/VCTS\nTuesday evening and gusty outflow winds. W'rly winds are expected\nto continue for the next few hours, then shift back E'rly or \nbecome VRB by 10-11Z. Winds will favor a S/SE direction through \nthe entire day with perhaps a brief period of W/SW winds in the \nevening, but confidence in a W/SW shift is low. Wind speeds \nwill mostly stay below 10 kts. SHRA/TSRA activity is expected to \ndevelop Tuesday afternoon and evening, but should mostly stay \nsouth and east of the metro with a low chance (10%) to spread \ninto the metro. Outflow winds should move into the metro from the \nS/SE with gusts up to 20-25 kts most likely and a low chance \n(5-10%) for 30+ kts. FEW to SCT clouds will be common through the \nTAF period with lowest bases around 8-10K ft AGL.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \n\nNo major aviation impacts are anticipated through the next 24 \nhours with mostly clear skies. Winds will favor W/NW at both KIPL\nand KBLH, with wind speeds mostly staying below 10 kts and periods\nof light VRB.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nNear to slightly above normal temperatures will prevail over the \nnext several days, with minimal day-to-day fluctuations. Dry \nconditions with no chances for rain continue through today ahead \nreturning high terrain rain chances for foothill and high terrain \nareas starting Tuesday. MinRHs between 15-30% will be common across \nthe region, with even higher readings (35-50%) for the far eastern \ndistricts. MaxRHs will range from fair to good over the western and \ncentral districts, to good and excellent for the eastern districts \ninto the weekend. Outside of some marginal breeziness caused by an \napproaching weather system, winds will follow familiar diurnal \ntrends with typical terrain induced afternoon gusts around 15-25 \nmph. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...18\nLONG TERM...RW \nAVIATION...Benedict\nFIRE WEATHER...RW\n",
